select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-b03-034","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"How does a wet-bulb thermometer differ from a dry-bulb thermometer, and which one reflects relative humidity?","back":"The wet bulb is wrapped in a wick soaked in distilled water. Evaporation absorbs latent heat and lowers its reading, so wet-bulb temperature reflects RH. The dry bulb measures sensible heat and is not influenced by RH.","explain":"The drier the air, the faster the evaporation and the bigger the wet-bulb depression. Enthalpy of moist air is read from the psychrometric chart using dry- and wet-bulb temperatures.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p96","order":1168},
{"id":"p1-b03-035","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"How is the calorific value of a fuel measured, and what makes up the difference between GCV and NCV?","back":"In a bomb calorimeter: a sealed, insulated vessel where a known mass is burnt completely and the temperature rise is measured. GCV − NCV = heat of vaporisation of the moisture and of the water formed from hydrogen in the fuel.","explain":"Typical heavy fuel oil values are GCV ≈ 10,500 kcal/kg and NCV ≈ 9,800 kcal/kg. The OCR text prints J/kg, but the figures (44,100 and 41,160) are kJ/kg. NCV assumes the water leaves as vapour, so its latent heat is not recovered.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p97","order":1169},
{"id":"p1-b03-036","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"In what unit is the rate of heat transfer measured, and name the three modes of heat transfer.","back":"Watts (J/s). The three modes are conduction (in solids), convection (fluid to/from a surface) and radiation (needs no medium).","explain":"Heat always flows from hot to cold. Energy transferred is in joules; the rate is in watts. An MCQ that offers 'kcal' or 'watts per second' for the rate is a trap.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p97","order":1170},
{"id":"p1-b03-037","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What are the two mechanisms of conduction, and how do forced and natural convection differ?","back":"Conduction: molecular motion (higher-energy molecules pass energy to neighbours) and migration of free electrons (mainly in pure metals). Forced convection uses a fan or pump to move the fluid; natural convection is driven by density differences, with hot fluid rising and cold fluid sinking.","explain":"Radiation needs no medium. Thermal radiation covers roughly infrared to ultraviolet wavelengths, and radiant energy hitting a surface can be reflected, absorbed or transmitted.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p98","order":1171},
{"id":"p1-b03-038","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"Using h_f = c_w (t_s − t₀) with c_w = 4.19 kJ/kg °C and t₀ = 0 °C, find the specific enthalpy of saturated water at atmospheric pressure (t_s = 100 °C). Given h_g = 2,676 kJ/kg, find the latent heat of evaporation.","back":"h_f ≈ 419 kJ/kg; h_fg = 2,676 − 419 = 2,257 kJ/kg.","explain":"Specific enthalpy of saturated water is sensible heat above 0 °C. Enthalpy of evaporation is the difference between saturated steam and saturated water enthalpies. At 1 atm these are the steam-table values 419, 2,676 and 2,257 kJ/kg.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p100","steps":[{"prompt":"Compute h_f.","answer":"4.19 × (100 − 0) = 419 kJ/kg","explain":"Sensible heat of water from the 0 °C reference."},{"prompt":"Write the evaporation enthalpy relation.","answer":"h_fg = h_g − h_f","explain":"Latent heat = steam enthalpy − water enthalpy."},{"prompt":"Compute h_fg.","answer":"2,676 − 419 = 2,257 kJ/kg","explain":"Matches the latent heat of vaporisation at 100 °C."}],"order":1172},
{"id":"p1-b03-039","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"How is the specific enthalpy of superheated steam calculated, and what specific heat does the guide book use?","back":"h_s = h_g + c_ps (t_s − t), where t is the saturation temperature and t_s the superheated temperature. c_ps ≈ 1.860 kJ/kg °C at standard atmosphere (it varies with temperature).","explain":"Superheat adds sensible heat to dry saturated steam. Specific enthalpy in general is h = u + p v (internal energy plus pressure × specific volume), and total enthalpy H = m h.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p101","order":1173},
{"id":"p1-b03-040","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"State the first, second and third laws of thermodynamics as given in the guide book.","back":"First: energy can be neither created nor destroyed, only converted or transferred (conservation of energy). Second: heat flows naturally only from hot to cold, and no heat engine can be 100% efficient. Third: it is impossible to reduce any system to absolute zero (−273 °C).","explain":"The second law introduces entropy, a measure of disorder: the more disorderly a system, the less useful work it can do. Some heat from the fuel must always be rejected to the surroundings, which is why heat engines are never 100% efficient.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p101","order":1174},
{"id":"p1-b03-041","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Express the derived SI units pascal, joule, watt, volt, ohm and lux in terms of other units.","back":"Pa = N/m²; J = N·m; W = J/s; V = W/A; Ω = V/A; lux = lm/m² (siemens = A/V).","explain":"Base units used in the table include metre, second, ampere, kelvin, mole and candela (plus kilogram). Derived units are built from them, for example a newton is kg·m/s².","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p102","order":1175},
{"id":"p1-b03-042","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Give the energy equivalents of 1 BTU, 1 HP and 1 MJ (in Wh) from the conversion table.","back":"1 BTU = 1,055 J = 252 cal; 1 HP = 745.7 W; 1 MJ = 278 Wh (1 Wh = 3,600 J).","explain":"1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J, which is why 1 MJ ≈ 0.278 kWh. HP × 0.746 converts motor horsepower to kW in the motor kVA formula.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p103","order":1176},
{"id":"p1-b03-043","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Per the Gazette notification of 19-03-2007, what standard calorific values are used for MTOE reporting for 1 kWh, furnace oil, HSD, petrol, LPG and charcoal?","back":"1 kWh = 860 kcal; furnace oil/RFO/LSHS/naphtha 10,050 kcal/kg (0.9337 kg/L); HSD 11,840 kcal/kg (0.8263 kg/L); petrol 11,200 kcal/kg (0.7087 kg/L); LPG 12,500 kcal/kg; charcoal 6,900 kcal/kg, unless the supplier's latest certificate states otherwise.","explain":"Coal and coke use the GCV from the supplier's latest certificate. Without certificates, a NABL-accredited, State or Government-recognised lab test is used. 1 kgoe = 10,000 kcal and 1 MTOE = 10⁷ kcal.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p104","order":1177},
{"id":"p1-b03-044","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"For MTOE reporting, what calorific value is used for natural gas if the supplier gives no certificate?","back":"The average of the 8,000–10,500 kcal/m³ range, about 9,250 kcal/m³.","explain":"The actual calorific value from the supplier's latest certificate is preferred. For gaseous fuels, MTOE = (quantity in kg or Nm³ × GCV in kcal/kg or kcal/Nm³) / 10⁷.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p104","order":1178},
{"id":"p1-b03-045","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"What is the oil equivalent of 125 t of coal with a GCV of 4,000 kcal/kg? (Chapter objective question 6)","back":"50 toe.","explain":"MTOE for solid fuel = (quantity in kg × GCV in kcal/kg) / 10⁷. Answer choices of 40, 400 and 500 are the usual unit-slip traps.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p104","steps":[{"prompt":"Convert the quantity to kg.","answer":"125 t = 125,000 kg","explain":"GCV is per kg."},{"prompt":"Compute the heat content.","answer":"125,000 × 4,000 = 5 × 10⁸ kcal","explain":"Energy = mass × GCV."},{"prompt":"Convert to toe.","answer":"5 × 10⁸ ÷ 10⁷ = 50 toe","explain":"1 toe = 10⁷ kcal."}],"order":1179},
{"id":"p1-043","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"How does the EC Act define an energy audit?","back":"The verification, monitoring and analysis of energy use, including a technical report with recommendations for improving energy efficiency, a cost-benefit analysis, and an action plan to reduce energy consumption.","explain":"The definition has three parts: measurement/analysis, recommendations with economics, and an action plan. An audit is only useful if it leads to implementation, which is why the report must quantify savings and costs.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1180},
{"id":"p1-044","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"What is the difference between a preliminary and a detailed energy audit?","back":"A preliminary (walk-through) audit is quick and uses existing data to set the consumption baseline, find obvious savings and no-cost or low-cost measures, and set priorities. A detailed audit is a comprehensive, instrument-based study of all major systems, with a full energy balance and costed projects.","explain":"A detailed audit is done in three phases: pre-audit, audit and post-audit. It gives the most accurate estimate of savings and costs, and includes a detailed project implementation plan.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1181},
{"id":"p1-045","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"What are the three phases of a detailed energy audit?","back":"Phase I: pre-audit (planning, walk-through, data collection plan). Phase II: audit (detailed measurement, analysis, energy and material balance, identifying options). Phase III: post-audit (implementing recommendations and following up on results).","explain":"Typical steps include a kick-off meeting with management, collecting historical data, preparing process flow charts, measurements, techno-economic evaluation of options, and the report with an action plan. Data collection, analysis and reporting are all steps of an audit.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q45","order":1182},
{"id":"p1-046","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Which energy audit instruments measure what: Fyrite, stroboscope, sling psychrometer, Pitot tube, ultrasonic flow meter, lux meter?","back":"Fyrite: O₂ or CO₂ in flue gas. Stroboscope: rotational speed without contact. Sling psychrometer: DBT and WBT (→ RH). Pitot tube: velocity pressure in ducts (→ air velocity and flow). Ultrasonic flow meter: liquid flow (transit-time or Doppler method). Lux meter: illuminance.","explain":"A power analyser measures kW, kVA, kVAr, PF, harmonics and other quantities directly. A plain ammeter or wattmeter does not give PF on its own. Choosing the right instrument is a frequent objective question.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q6, Q46, S8; BEE 2025 P1 Q39","order":1183},
{"id":"p1-047","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Define Specific Energy Consumption (SEC).","back":"SEC = Energy consumed ÷ Production in the same period, e.g. kWh/t or kcal/kg of product. Example: 5 t of coal at 4,000 kcal/kg for 25 t of cement → 20×10⁶ ÷ 25,000 kg = 800 kcal/kg.","explain":"SEC is the main plant-level energy performance indicator used for benchmarking and for PAT targets. Compare SEC only at similar capacity utilisation and product mix, because fixed energy is spread over output.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q36","order":1184},
{"id":"p1-048","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"How is plant energy performance calculated?","back":"Production factor = current year production ÷ reference year production. Reference year equivalent = reference year energy × production factor. Plant energy performance (%) = (reference year equivalent − current year energy) ÷ reference year equivalent × 100.","explain":"A positive value means the plant used less energy than expected for its output, so efficiency improved. A negative value means it got worse. The calculation needs current and reference year production and the reference year energy; capacity utilisation is not needed.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q4, L4","order":1185},
{"id":"p1-049","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"What is benchmarking in energy management?","back":"Comparing a plant's energy performance (SEC or other indicators) with its own past performance (internal) or with similar plants or best practice (external), to find improvement potential.","explain":"Fair benchmarking needs comparable boundaries, product mix, capacity utilisation, raw material quality, technology vintage and climate. PAT targets are set by benchmarking each plant's SEC against others in its sector.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1186},
{"id":"p1-050","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"What does a Sankey diagram show, and why is it useful?","back":"It shows energy flows through a system as arrows whose widths are proportional to the energy quantity, so inputs, useful output and losses can be seen at a glance.","explain":"Large loss streams stand out visually, which helps the energy manager prioritise where to act. It is a qualitative and quantitative presentation of an energy balance; it does not replace calculations or audits.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q38","order":1187},
{"id":"p1-051","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"problem","front":"Reference year (2023) data: production 50,000 t, energy use 30,000 toe. Current year (2024): production 55,000 t, energy use 31,500 toe. Calculate the plant energy performance for 2024 and interpret it.","back":"Plant energy performance = +4.55%, so the plant used 4.55% less energy than expected for its production (an improvement).","explain":"The production factor scales reference-year energy to what the plant would have used at this year's output. A positive result means the plant performed better than the reference year. A negative result, as in the BEE 2024 question (−5.56%), means performance got worse.","steps":[{"prompt":"Calculate the production factor.","answer":"PF = 55,000 ÷ 50,000 = 1.10","explain":"PF = current year production ÷ reference year production."},{"prompt":"Calculate the reference year equivalent energy.","answer":"30,000 × 1.10 = 33,000 toe","explain":"Expected energy at current output = reference energy × PF."},{"prompt":"Calculate plant energy performance.","answer":"(33,000 − 31,500) ÷ 33,000 × 100 = +4.55%","explain":"PEP = (Ref. eq. − Current energy) ÷ Ref. eq. × 100."},{"prompt":"Interpret the result.","answer":"Positive value: the plant saved 1,500 toe compared with expected consumption.","explain":"A negative value would mean worse performance."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 L4(b) (modified)","order":1188},
{"id":"p1-b04-001","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Give the BEE guide book definition of energy management.","back":"The judicious and effective use of energy to maximise profits (minimise costs) and enhance competitive positions.","explain":"An alternative definition in the book is the strategy of adjusting and optimising energy, using systems and procedures, to reduce energy requirements per unit of output while holding constant or reducing total costs. The fundamental goal is to produce goods and provide services with the least cost and least environmental effect.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p109","order":1189},
{"id":"p1-b04-002","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"What are the three objectives of energy management?","back":"1) Achieve and maintain optimum energy procurement and utilisation throughout the organisation; 2) minimise energy costs/waste without affecting production and quality; 3) minimise environmental effects.","explain":"Note that minimising cost must not come at the expense of production or quality. An exam MCQ lists minimising energy costs, minimising waste and minimising environmental degradation, and the answer is 'all the above'.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p109","order":1190},
{"id":"p1-b04-003","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Which three costs are often the top operating costs in industry, and which has the highest potential for cost reduction?","back":"Energy (electrical and thermal), labour and materials. Energy has the highest potential for cost reduction.","explain":"This is the book's justification for the need for an energy audit. An audit reviews energy costs, availability and reliability of supply, the appropriate energy mix, conservation technologies and retrofits. The book describes the audit as translating conservation ideas into realities within a specified time.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p110","order":1191},
{"id":"p1-b04-004","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Name the three types of energy audit in the BEE guide book.","back":"Preliminary audit, targeted energy audit and detailed audit.","explain":"The type chosen depends on the type of industry, the depth of audit needed and the potential and magnitude of cost reduction desired. A preliminary audit is quick and uses existing data. A targeted audit studies specific systems. A detailed audit covers all major energy-using equipment.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p110","order":1192},
{"id":"p1-b04-005","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"By what two other names is a preliminary energy audit known?","back":"Walk-through audit and diagnostic audit.","explain":"It is a relatively quick exercise that uses existing or easily obtained data, such as energy bills, invoices and production records. Its outputs include an estimate of the savings scope, immediate no-/low-cost measures, a baseline and areas for more detailed study.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p110","order":1193},
{"id":"p1-b04-006","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"List the scope of a preliminary energy audit.","back":"Establish energy consumption (from bills); obtain related production data; estimate the scope for savings; identify the easiest areas for attention; identify immediate no-/low-cost savings; set up a baseline; identify areas for detailed study.","explain":"The baseline or reference point set up in a preliminary audit is used later to measure progress. Examples of the 'easiest areas' are unnecessary lighting, higher temperature settings and leaks.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p110","order":1194},
{"id":"p1-b04-007","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Classify these as no-cost or low-cost measures: arresting steam/air leaks; shutting idle motors; adjusting the fan damper to control excess air; replacing lamps with appropriate lamps and luminaires.","back":"No-cost: arresting leaks, controlling excess air by damper adjustment. Low-cost: shutting equipment when not needed, replacing lamps and luminaires.","explain":"The book lists, as areas needing detailed study rather than quick fixes: converting direct to indirect steam heating with condensate recovery, upgrading insulation, modifying the process to reduce steam demand, rescheduling operations to cut peak demand, and evaluating waste heat recovery.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p110","order":1195},
{"id":"p1-b04-008","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Why does a detailed energy audit give the most accurate estimate of savings, and what is its key element?","back":"It accounts for the energy use of all major equipment and considers the interactive effects of various projects. Its key element is the energy balance.","explain":"The energy balance is based on an inventory of energy-using systems, assumptions of current operating conditions, and measurements and calculations of energy use. A detailed audit includes detailed savings calculations and project implementation costs, and results in a detailed project implementation plan.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p111","order":1196},
{"id":"p1-b04-009","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"In BEE's ten-step detailed audit methodology, which steps fall in each phase?","back":"Pre-audit phase: steps 1–2 (plan and organise; introductory meeting). Audit phase: steps 3–9 (data gathering to reporting and presentation). Post-audit phase: step 10 (implementation and follow-up).","explain":"The audit phase covers: 3 primary data gathering, process flow and utility diagrams; 4 survey and monitoring; 5 detailed trials; 6 energy use analysis; 7 identifying ENCON opportunities; 8 cost-benefit analysis; 9 reporting to top management. The book says the methodology is flexible and can be adapted to the industry.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p111","order":1197},
{"id":"p1-b04-010","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Which detailed trials are done in Step 5 of the ten-step methodology, and how long does a boiler efficiency trial last?","back":"24-hour power monitoring (MD, PF, kWh), load variation trends in pumps, fans and compressors, boiler efficiency trials (4–8 hours), furnace efficiency trials and equipment performance tests.","explain":"Step 4 is the broader survey with portable instruments (motors, insulation, lighting), where operating data are confirmed against design data. Step 5 is a focused set of trials on selected major energy equipment.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p112","order":1198},
{"id":"p1-b04-011","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"How long should the initial site visit of an energy audit take, and what should it achieve?","back":"About one day. It should finalise the audit team, learn management's expectations, identify the main energy-consuming areas and the instrumentation needed, plan the time frame, collect macro data and build support.","explain":"During the visit the auditor discusses the aims and economic guidelines of the audit with senior management, analyses major energy data, obtains site drawings and tours the site with a site representative. Proper planning is a prerequisite for an effective audit.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p113","order":1199},
{"id":"p1-b04-012","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Name the baseline data an energy audit team should collect.","back":"Quantity and type of raw materials; technology, process and equipment used; capacity utilisation; efficiencies/yield; % rejection/reprocessing; quantity and types of wastes; consumption of fuel, water, steam, electricity, compressed air, cooling and chilled water.","explain":"The auditor should specially interview supervisors and equipment operators. The maintenance manager is often the primary person to ask about lighting types, motor sizes, the A/C plant, electrical load and related performance problems.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p114","order":1200},
{"id":"p1-b04-013","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Under which of the four ENCON areas (fuel substitution, energy generation, energy distribution, energy usage by processes) does the major opportunity usually lie?","back":"Energy usage by processes. Many of these opportunities are hidden, and process analysis is a useful tool for process integration measures.","explain":"Energy generation covers DG sets, boilers, cogeneration and gasifiers. Energy distribution covers transformers, cables, PF improvement and chilled water, cooling water and compressed air networks. Fuel substitution means choosing the appropriate fuel for efficient conversion.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p115","order":1201},
{"id":"p1-b04-014","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"Which issues should the technical feasibility of an energy-efficiency measure address?","back":"Technology availability, space and skilled manpower; impact on safety, quality, production or process; reliability, service, maintenance requirements and spares availability.","explain":"Economic viability is often the key parameter for management acceptance. It can be assessed by payback, IRR or NPV. For low-investment, short-duration measures, the payback method is sufficient.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p116","order":1202},
{"id":"p1-b04-015","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"From BEE's economic feasibility worksheet, how are net savings and payback period (in months) calculated?","back":"Net savings/year = annual savings − annual operating costs. Payback (months) = investment ÷ net savings per year × 12.","explain":"On the worksheet, investment covers equipment, civil works, instrumentation and auxiliaries. Annual operating costs include cost of capital, maintenance, manpower, energy and depreciation. Annual savings cover thermal energy, electrical energy, raw materials and waste disposal.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p116","order":1203},
{"id":"p1-b04-016","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"What are the three categories of ENCON measures, and which normally gets priority?","back":"Low cost–high return, medium cost–medium return, and high cost–high return. Low cost–high return projects normally get priority.","explain":"Process changes almost always involve high costs with high returns. They need careful scrutiny before funds are committed, are complex, and need long lead times before implementation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p116","order":1204},
{"id":"p1-b04-017","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"In BEE's project priority guideline (Table 4.1), what do priorities A, B, C and D mean?","back":"A – Good (well defined, attractive, existing technology adequate, no risk). B – May be (marginally acceptable, minor risk). C – Held (poorly defined, marginally unacceptable, doubtful). D – No (clearly not attractive, needs major breakthrough, not feasible).","explain":"Each project is rated on economic, technical and risk feasibility. A 'C' project has inadequate existing technology. A 'D' project needs a major technological breakthrough and is not feasible.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p117","order":1205},
{"id":"p1-b04-018","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"How should an energy audit report begin, and who is that part written for?","back":"With an executive summary giving a brief synopsis of total savings and the highlights of each measure. It should be tailored to non-technical personnel.","explain":"The main report then describes the process, presents annual consumption and bills, describes energy inputs and outputs by department and gives the ENCON measures with costs, benefits and payback. It concludes with recommendations for detailed engineering and feasibility studies for high-investment measures.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p117","order":1206},
{"id":"p1-b04-019","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"problem","front":"A main cooling tower is rated 5,000 m³/h at 8 °C design range but runs at 3,000 m³/h with a 4 °C range. A separate DG-set cooling tower handles 240 m³/h at 5 °C range and has 2 × 7.5 kW pumps and 2 × 22 kW fans running at 80% load. Merging the DG flow into the main tower needs about 7 kW extra pumping. Find the net savings, annual savings (8,400 h, Rs 4/kWh) and payback on Rs 1.5 lakh of piping.","back":"Net saving 40 kW → 3,36,000 kWh/yr → about Rs 13.4 lakh/yr; payback under 2 months.","explain":"This is the book's sample of the reporting format for an ENCON recommendation (Table 4.4). It shows that the main tower is only 30% loaded, so it can absorb the small DG-set heat load and the separate tower can be stopped.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p120","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the % loading of the main cooling tower.","answer":"(3,000 × 4) ÷ (5,000 × 8) = 12,000 ÷ 40,000 = 30%","explain":"Loading is the actual heat load (flow × range) over the design heat load."},{"prompt":"Find the heat load of the DG-set cooling tower.","answer":"240 × 1,000 × 1 × 5 = 12,00,000 kcal/h","explain":"Heat load = water mass (kg/h) × Cp (1 kcal/kg °C) × range."},{"prompt":"Find the power drawn by the DG-set tower.","answer":"(2 × 22 + 2 × 7.5) × 0.80 = 59 × 0.8 ≈ 47 kW","explain":"Both fans and both pumps are taken at 80% load."},{"prompt":"Find the net power saving.","answer":"47 − 7 = 40 kW","explain":"The book estimates about 7 kW of extra pumping at the main tower for the added 240 m³/h (66.67 L/s)."},{"prompt":"Find the annual kWh and cost savings.","answer":"40 × 8,400 = 3,36,000 kWh/yr; × Rs 4 = Rs 13.44 lakh/yr","explain":"Units saved × tariff gives the cost saving."},{"prompt":"Find the simple payback.","answer":"1.5 ÷ 13.44 × 12 ≈ 1.3 months, i.e. less than 2 months","explain":"Only piping is needed, so the investment is very small."}],"order":1207},
{"id":"p1-b04-020","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"True or false: energy costs are a fixed overhead with little scope for savings.","back":"False. Contrary to common belief, energy costs are not a fixed overhead, and there is often huge potential for savings.","explain":"Understanding energy costs is vital for awareness creation and saving calculations. Where meters are insufficient, fuel and electricity invoices and the annual company balance sheet (fuel and power cost with production data) are useful sources.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 04 p121","order":1208}
]$cards$::jsonb);
